    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d339">

                <pb facs="0486" n="404" sameAs="frus:frus1925v02/pg404"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.6176 F 51/7: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Clerk in Charge of the
                        Legation at Monrovia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wall</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-01-07T16:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 7,
                                1925—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>1. Your January 2, 3 p.m.<anchor n="12" xml:id="d339-f1"/> Firestone telephones
                    Department from Akron that he is telegraphing his agents in Liberia and also
                    President King that contracts are on way and he hopes legislative action may be
                    taken as soon as they arrive. He believes that contracts will meet President’s
                    approval and is very anxious that matter be settled as he desires to begin
                    work.</p>
                <p>Firestone says he has information that British interests are becoming active in
                    opposition and that proposed act changing Customs Tariff may be part of their
                    propaganda. If so requested Legation may give appropriate support to the
                    American interests concerned. Please show this telegram in strict confidence to
                    De la Rue who has <pb facs="0487" n="405" xml:id="pg405"/> often discussed
                    proposed contracts with President King. Hood sailing immediately.<anchor n="13"
                        xml:id="d339-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d339-f1" n="12" xml:id="d339-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d339-f2" n="13" xml:id="d339-f2-content">
                <p>Solomon Porter Hood, Minister Resident and Consul General in Liberia (Oct. 26,
                    1921 to Aug. 31, 1926), temporarily in the United States.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
